// Raw-ingredient content — what the factory's *consumables* cost
// (GAMEPLAY.md §5's first cost axis: "ingredients — recurring, scales
// directly with production volume"). Data, never hardcoded in sim_core,
// exactly like `recipes.ron`, `machines.ron`, `shop.ron` and
// `contracts.ron`; the loader is `sim_core::sim::ingredients::
// IngredientCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way every other data file's loader does.
//
// WHY THIS IS A SECOND FILE AND NOT A COLUMN IN `shop.ron`. They price two
// different *kinds of thing*, and the difference is not cosmetic. A
// `shop.ron` entry prices a **buildable kind** — something you buy once,
// place on the floor, and get back if you pick it up; buying one credits
// `OwnedEquipment`. An entry here prices a **consumable item or fluid** —
// something you buy by the unit, load into a hopper or a tank, and never
// see again once a machine has eaten it; buying one credits
// `OwnedIngredients`. The two ledgers are separate for the same reason
// `OwnedEquipment` is not an `Inventory`: they share a shape and not a
// meaning, and one file with a "kind" discriminator would only make the
// distinction a runtime check instead of a type.
//
// WHAT COUNTS AS RAW. An id here is an item or fluid that some recipe in
// `data/recipes.ron` *consumes* and no recipe *produces* — tomato and sugar
// on the solid side, vinegar on the fluid side. Everything else in the chain
// (tomato paste, the liquid mix, the cooked ketchup, bottles, boxes) is made
// by the factory and therefore is never bought. `IngredientCatalog::
// validate_covers` checks that direction against the shipped recipe file, so
// a new raw ingredient with no price is caught by a test rather than
// discovered as a hopper that can never be filled. It is deliberately NOT a
// `World`-construction panic — an unpriced ingredient leaves the world
// perfectly coherent, it just cannot be bought, which is the same call
// `contracts.rs` made about an unsatisfiable contract.
//
// A SOLID AND A FLUID SHARE ONE LEDGER, ON PURPOSE. `ItemId` and `FluidId`
// are both plain `String` aliases (`sim/types.rs`), the ids never collide,
// and what the player is buying in both cases is *stock measured in whole
// units*. One catalog and one ledger is therefore one mechanism, not two
// that have to be kept in step — and a shipment of vinegar is no more a
// different kind of purchase than a shipment of sugar.
//
// PRICES ARE PLACEHOLDERS, like `shop.ron`'s (GAMEPLAY.md §10 puts real
// balance in a future pass). What they encode is a shape:
//
//   1. One boxed ketchup — the only thing the chain sells — costs
//      4 tomato + 1 sugar + 2 vinegar to make, i.e. 4 + 3 + 4 = 11 in
//      ingredients.
//   2. The shipped contract board pays between 24 and 30 per box. So
//      ingredients are roughly a third of revenue: a running factory is
//      comfortably profitable, but overproducing into a delivery pool no
//      contract wants is a real waste of money rather than a free
//      accumulation.
//   3. Tomatoes are the cheapest per unit and are consumed four at a time,
//      which makes them the biggest line on the bill — bulk produce
//      behaving like bulk produce. Sugar is the dearest per unit (it is
//      refined) and the smallest line.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    entries: [
        // The chain's bulk raw produce. Cheapest per unit and consumed four
        // at a time by the crusher.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "tomato",
            price: 1,
        ),
        // Refined, so the dearest of the three per unit — but the mixer only
        // takes one per batch.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "sugar",
            price: 3,
        ),
        // The one raw ingredient that is a fluid rather than an item: it is
        // bought by the unit exactly like the two solids and loaded into a
        // `fluid_tank` instead of a hopper.
        IngredientEntry(
            id: "vinegar",
            price: 2,
        ),
    ],
)
